{
  "case_id": "2011-11-398",
  "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Department of Health and Human Services",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Affordable Care Act addresses a fundamental and enduring problem in our health care system and our economy. Insurance has become the predominant means of paying for health care in this country. Insurance has become the predominant means of paying for health care in this country. For most Americans, for more than 80 percent of Americans, the insurance system does provide effective access. Excuse me."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why aren't those problems that the Federal Government can address directly?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Department of Health and Human Services",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under the Commerce Clause, what Congress has done is to enact reforms of the insurance market, directed at the individual insurance market, that preclude -- that preclude discrimination based on pre-existing conditions, that require guaranteed issue and community rating."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Do you think there is a market for burial services?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I can't imagine that that -- that the Commerce Clause would -- would forbid Congress from taking into account this deeply embedded social norm."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The second part of our argument is that the means here that Congress has chosen, the minimum coverage provision, is a means that regulates the -- that regulates economic activity, namely your transaction in the health care market, with substantial effects on interstate commerce;"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "authority to impose an insurance requirement or other -- or other penalty at the point of sale, and they have agreed that Congress has the authority to do that to achieve the same objectives that the minimum coverage provision in the Affordable Care Act is designed to achieve."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "what Congress chose to do was to rely on market mechanisms and efficiency and a method that has more choice than would the traditional Medicare or Medicaid type model."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you acknowledge that that's a principle?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Whatever the States have chosen not to do, the Federal Government can do?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "in this context, the subsidizers eventually become the subsidized?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Can I tell you what the something else is so -- while you're answering it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I have looked for a case that involves the issue of whether something denominated by Congress as a penalty was nevertheless treated as a tax, except in those situations where the code itself or the statute itself said treat the penalty as a tax."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: I suppose, though, General, one question is whether the determined efforts of Congress not to refer to this as a tax make a difference."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, Mr. Clement, now it seems as though you're just talking about a matter of timing, that Congress can regulate the transaction. And the question is when does it make best sense to regulate that transaction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, Justice Kagan, we don't think it's a matter of timing alone, and we think it has very significant substantive effects, because if Congress tried to regulate at the point of sale, the one group that it wouldn't capture at all are the people who don't want to purchase health insurance and also have no plans of using health care services in the near term. And Congress very much wanted to capture those people."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Clement, doesn't that work -- that work the way Social Security does? Let me put it this way: Congress, in the '30s, saw a real problem of people needing to have old age and survivor's insurance. And, yes, they did it through a tax, but they said everybody has got to be in it because if we don't have the healthy in it, there's not going to be the money to pay for the ones who become old or disabled or widowed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could we have an exemption? Could the government say everybody pays a shared health care responsibility payment to offset all the money that we're forced to spend on health care, we the government; but anybody who has an insurance policy is exempt from that tax? Could the government do that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Let me ask -- can I go back for a step? Because I don't want to get into a discussion of whether this is a good bill or not. Some people think it's going to save a lot of money. Some people think it won't. So, I'm focusing just on the Commerce Clause; not on the Due Process Clause, the Commerce Clause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the government -- this statute undeniably operates in the health care insurance market. And the government can't say that everybody is in that market. The whole problem is that everybody is not in that market, and they want to make everybody get into that market."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0003-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 70,
        "start": 70
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But, Mr. Clement, this goes back to the Chief Justice's question. But, of course, the theory behind, not just the Government's case, but the theory behind this law is that people are in this market right now, and they're in this market because people do get sick, and because when people get sick, we provide them with care without making them pay."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0004-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 75,
        "start": 75
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: Justice Kagan, again, with all due respect, I don't think that's a limiting principle. My unwillingness to buy an electric car is forcing up the price of an electric car. If only more people demanded an electric car, there would be economies of scale, and the price would go down."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0004-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 76,
        "start": 76
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And that gets at the idea that there's two kinds of cost shifting that are going on here. One is the concern about emergency care and that somehow somebody who gets sick is going to shift costs back to other policy areas -- holders. But there's a much bigger cost shifting going on here, and that's the cost shifting that goes on when you force healthy people into an insurance market precisely because they're healthy, precisely because they're not likely to go to the emergency room, precisely because they're not likely to use the insurance they're forced to buy in the health care insurance. That creates a huge windfall. It lowers the price of premiums."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 81,
        "start": 81
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Could you tell me, do you think the States could pass this mandate?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0004-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Is your answer to that yes or no?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Carvin",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0005-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I agree with you that since the government regulates all markets, there is no limiting principle on their compelled purchase."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0005-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 97,
        "start": 97
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So, how is that different from saying you are self-insuring today, you're forgoing insurance? Why isn't that a predecessor to the need that you're eventually going to have?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Carvin",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0005-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 104,
        "start": 104
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The difference is that the taxpayer is not given a choice. It's the difference between banning cigarettes and saying I'm going to enforce that legal ban through a $5 a pack penalty, and saying, look, if you want to sell cigarettes, fine; I'm going to charge you a tax of $5 a pack."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0005-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 109,
        "start": 109
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you think that drawing a line between commerce and everything else that is not commerce is drawing an artificial line, like drawing a line between commerce and manufacturing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0005-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 110,
        "start": 110
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress confronted a grave problem when it enacted the Affordable Care Act: the 40 million Americans who can't get health insurance and suffered often very terrible consequences."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Counsel",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
      "evidence_id": "ev-7f9c90f69995-0006-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 111,
        "start": 111
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That is the kind of choice of means that McCulloch says that the Constitution leaves to the democratically accountable branches of government."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2011-11-398",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "7f9c90f69995f5bc79e01c3baa3066c820a42f4edeaa1e8cfdf61a7635165175",
